  "reference": "THE CROSS-LANGUAGE PROOF for the machine-learning surface, the counterpart of fixtures/estimation/fit_cross_language.json, fixtures/toolbox/toolbox_cross_language.json and fixtures/callback/callback_cross_language.json. Unlike its sibling fixtures/ml/machine_learning.json -- whose job is pinning ACCURACY against the upstream MSTest literals, so its tolerances are the loose Assert.AreEqual deltas those tests use (1e-2 for the GMM's mclust comparison, 1e-6 for k-means) -- every assertion here is at ZERO tolerance (mode \"equal\"), and its job is the guarantee both packages make to their users: a seeded fit produces bit-identical numbers in C++, R, Python and the real C# library, because every operation happens in the shared compiled core and the PRNG is the same bit-exact Mersenne Twister. Three seeded fits cover the three ways randomness enters this layer. `kmeans_seeded` exercises the k-means++ initialization stream (one uniform draw per centre plus a weighted CDF walk). `random_forest_seeded` exercises the two-generator bootstrap pattern -- all tree seeds drawn up front from one generator, then each tree resampling from its own -- which is what makes upstream's Parallel.For reproducible and this port's serial loop identical to it. `knn_intervals_seeded` exercises the bootstrap-resample-per-realization stream, and pins the lower/median/upper columns but NOT the mean column: that one comes from Statistics.ParallelMean, whose PLINQ partitioned sum makes the C# value depend on the machine's core count (measured: it diverges from a serial sum at every size from n = 16 up -- see docs/upstream-csharp-issues.md). The port sums serially, so R and Python agree with each other bit for bit on that column while C# need not; asserting it at zero tolerance would pin a number upstream cannot reproduce against itself, so it is OMITTED rather than given a loosened tolerance or an oracle_skip mask. Every expected value below was dumped from the REAL C# classes via tools/oracle_emitter and is re-verified by tools/verify_oracles.py. ONE FURTHER OMISSION, measured rather than assumed. `knn_intervals_seeded` also omits flat index 4 (row 1's lower bound). MEASURED on this machine (Apple clang 21, arm64): the C# value is 72.74699537750385 and the C++/R/Python value is 72.746995377503836, a 2 ULP difference, and compiling the same probe with -ffp-contract=off reproduces the C# value exactly -- so the cause is fused multiply-add contraction inside the shared core, in `tools::distance`'s `d += dx * dx`, which clang and gcc fuse by default and .NET never does. The other five pinned values in that case are unaffected. This is NOT the callback-catalog situation the -ffp-contract=off flag in core/CMakeLists.txt addresses: that flag deliberately reaches only fixture_callback_catalog.cpp, because the ported core must be compiled the same way the shipped R and Python packages compile it, and they pass no such flag. So the R-to-Python guarantee is intact here (both build the core identically) while the C#-to-package guarantee is 2 ULP on this one value, and the assertion is omitted rather than masked with oracle_skip or given a loosened tolerance.",
